face":"சான்ற","parts":[{"text":"சான்ற","lemma":"சான்ற","gloss":"abundant with"}],"gloss":"abundant with","split_unverified":true},{"surface":"மைந்தினோய்","parts":[{"text":"மைந்து","lemma":"மைந்து","gloss":"strength"},{"text":"இன்","lemma":"இன்","gloss":"oblique case marker"},{"text":"ஓய்","lemma":"ஓய்","gloss":"vocative suffix"}],"gloss":"O strong one!"},{"surface":"நினக்கே","parts":[{"text":"நினக்கே","lemma":"நினக்கே","gloss":"to you alone"}],"gloss":"to you alone","split_unverified":true}]}],"literal":"Where swift chariots rutted the broad streets / yoking a herd of white-mouthed donkeys / you ruined their wide, good fortresses. / Where flocks of birds chirp in the famous fertile fields / white-maned spirited horses with curved hooves leap / you drove your chariots in your enemies' land. / With swaying gait, thick necks / and broad feet, with furious looks / male elephants with gleaming tusks plunged into their protected ponds. / Since you are of such wrathful nature, / holding a beauty-shining shield inlaid with bright gold / and raising a long, gleaming spear, those enemies / who came with bright weapons and swift vanguards to confront you / had their desires left behind, / and those who lived in disgrace, are they many? On the other hand, / following the good treatises of the four Vedas / with rare fame and great offerings / where ghee-abundant oblations overflowed, many times / completing sacrifices of unfailing excellence / and erecting sacrificial posts, are those wide grounds many? / Which of them are more numerous, O Great One? The leather-strapped / tightly bound clay-smeared drum / to which the songstress sings of Vañci / is sought after by you, who are filled with strength!","translation":"O Great King! You yoked white-mouthed donkeys to plow and ruin the wide, beautiful fortresses of your enemies where swift chariots once rutted the streets. In their fertile fields, where birds once chirped, your spirited, white-maned horses now leap, and your war elephants plunge into their guarded ponds. Because of your fierce wrath, those enemies who marched against you with bright weapons and swift vanguards were defeated, their hopes shattered, and many lived on in disgrace. Yet, in your own land, you perform glorious sacrifices of unfailing excellence according to the four Vedas, offering abundant ghee and erecting sacrificial posts in wide arenas. Which of these are more numerous, O King: the ruined lands of your enemies, or the sacred grounds of your sacrifices? Your great strength is praised in songs of victory by songstresses to the beat of clay-smeared drums!","modern":"வேந்தனே! உன்னுடைய பகைவர்களின் அகன்ற நல்ல கோட்டைகளை, முன்பு தேர்கள் ஓடிய தெருக்களில் வெள்வாய்க் கழுதைகளைப் பூட்டி உழுது நீ பாழாக்கினாய். பறவைகள் ஒலிக்கும் அவர்களுடைய வளமான வயல்களில் உன்னுடைய குதிரைகள் பாய்ந்து ஓடத் தேர்களைச் செலுத்தினாய். அவர்களுடைய காவல் மிகுந்த குளங்களில் உன்னுடைய யானைகளை இறக்கிப் பாழாக்கினாய். இத்தகைய சினத்தை உடையவன் நீ. அதனால், உன்னை எதிர்க்க வந்த பகைவர்கள் தோற்று அவமானமடைந்தனர். அதே நேரத்தில், நீ நான்கு வேதங்களின்படி நெய் சொரியும் வேள்விகளைச் செய்து யூபத் தூண்களை நட்டாய். உன்னால் அழிக்கப்பட்ட பகைவர் நாடுகள் பலவா? அல்லது நீ வேள்வி செய்த இடங்கள் பலவா? வஞ்சிப் பாடல்களைப் பாடினி பாட, முழவு முழங்க, வலிமை மிக்க உன்னையே நாடிப் பாடுகிறோம்.","explanation":"This poem belongs to the Pāṭāṇ tiṇai (praising a king's greatness) and Iyaṉmoḻi tuṟai (describing the king's natural qualities). The poet Kapilar addresses the Cēra king Celvak Kaṭuṅkō Vāḻiyātaṉ, contrasting his destructive fury in war with his righteous piety in peace. Kapilar describes how the king ruined his enemies' lands by plowing their streets with donkeys and letting his horses and elephants ravage their fields and ponds. In contrast, the king's own land is filled with sacred arenas where grand Vedic sacrifices are performed and sacrificial posts are erected. The poet rhetorically asks which are more numerous: the lands the king destroyed or the sacrificial grounds he established. The poem beautifully captures the dual nature of a classical Tamil king—fierce to his enemies but deeply righteous and generous to his subjects.","model":"gemini-3.5-flash"}}